    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d328">

                <pb facs="0360" n="300" sameAs="frus:frus1895p1/pg300"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Eustis to Mr.
                            Olney</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1895-12-05"><hi rendition="#italic">December 5,
                        1895</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>I concur fully in your statement of the law of the case. I have always had in
                    view the danger of attempting to establish a precedent which might be used
                    against us. My contention for a copy of the evidence has been based exclusively
                    upon the exceptional circumstances under which Waller was tried, which I
                    maintained was different from a case where there was a regular and orderly
                    administration of justice. As to the use to have been made of the evidence, if
                    communicated, I <pb facs="0361" n="301" xml:id="pg301"/> agree with you. I
                    informed Mr. Hanotaux that my Government could not enter into a controversy with
                    the French Government as to the weight and the effect of evidence adduced before
                    a legally constituted tribunal.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Eustis</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
